.TH SYNCTANG\-KEYHOLDER 1 "" "synctang" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
synctang\-keyholder \- the laptop key holder for synctang
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B synctang\-keyholder \-\-version
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder pair
.RI [ flags ]
.I link
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder init
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder unlock
.RI [ flags ]
.IR machine\-name | machine\-transport\-id
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder machines
.RB [ list ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder machines add
.RI [ flags ]
.I name transport\-id
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder machines remove
.I name
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder status
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder completion
.I shell
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B synctang\-keyholder
holds a long-term private key and answers a machine's recovery attempts on
demand.
The machine runs
.BR synctang\-unlocker (8)
and waits at its LUKS prompt; the key holder always dials, never listens.
It looks the machine up by its transport identity, shows the machine's name and
identity, waits for a person to approve, answers once, and reports how it went.
.PP
The private key stays on this computer.
The machine recovers the volume secret from the answer, checks that it opens
the volume, and only then uses it.
By default the public Syncthing relay and discovery servers are used, and on the
same local network the machine is found by multicast; see
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5)
to use your own discovery server.
.PP
.B \-\-version
(also spelled
.BR \-version )
as the only argument prints the program's name and version, and the licence, and exits with status 0.
In a package the version is the one
.BR dpkg (1)
shows; a program built any other way says that it is a development build.
.PP
.BR \-\-color =\fIwhen\fR
(also spelled
.BR \-color ),
anywhere on the command line, chooses whether the output of a command is coloured and decorated for a person at a
terminal:
.B auto
(the default) does it only when standard output or standard error is a terminal,
.B always
also when it is not (to a pager, say), and
.B never
not at all.
What is added is colour (errors red, warnings yellow, what went well green), check marks and crosses, a few emoji,
tables with a header, a frame around the code to compare, and a line that turns while the program waits.
Each symbol has the words it stands for, which are what a locale that is not UTF-8, the Linux console and a
terminal of type
.B dumb
get, and to anything that is not a terminal the output is the plain text it has always been, which scripts read.
Prose is broken to the width of the terminal.
.PP
.B \-\-verbose
(also spelled
.BR \-verbose ),
anywhere on the command line, or
.B SYNCTANG_DEBUG=1
in the environment, prints a debug log to standard error: which store the record names for the key file,
where the key was read from (a path, or the name of the Secret Service item and its account), and whether its
public key matched the one recorded when it was made.
It never prints the key.
.SH COMMANDS
Every command accepts
.B \-h
and prints its flags and exits with status 2.
Flags may be written with one or two dashes.
.SS pair
Enrols this key holder on a machine from the pairing link that
.B synctang\-unlocker pair
prints under its QR code.
That one string is all that is copied: it carries the machine's transport identity, its name, the
discovery server if it is not the public one, and a one-time code.
Quote it, because it contains
.BR & .
.PP
If there is no key yet, one is made first, as
.B init
would, in the store
.B \-\-store
names.
The key holder then dials the machine and sends its public key; the machine answers with a random challenge,
which it chooses only after it has the key, and the key holder proves it holds the code.
It then prints a six-digit code, for example
.BR "483 291" .
The machine shows its operator the same code with the question whether to enrol this key holder.
.B Compare them.
The one-time code only proves that whoever answered had it, and a link that was photographed, shown
over a shoulder or pasted somewhere is had by more than one party: if the codes differ, somebody else
used the link first, and the operator should answer no.
.PP
When the operator confirms, the machine writes a keyslot and the command exits 0 after saving the
machine's name (from the link, or
.BR \-\-as )
in the file that
.B machines
manages, so that
.B synctang\-keyholder unlock
.I name
works.
A name that already means another machine is not taken over unless
.B \-\-force
is given, and that is checked before anything is sent.
A name in the link that cannot be used as a name here does not stop the pairing; there is then just no name to save.
The passphrase never leaves the machine.
.B synctang\-unlocker enrol
remains for the cases pairing cannot reach.
.TP
\fB\-\-store\fR \fIstore\fR
If a key has to be made: where to keep it; see
.BR init .
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
The name this key holder gives itself, shown on the machine.
Default: this computer's host name.
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fIname\fR
The name to unlock this machine by.
Default: the name in the link; with neither, no name is saved and the command gives the transport
identity to unlock by.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Let the name stand for this machine even if it names another one now.
Without it, that is refused before the machine is dialled.
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
Give up reaching the machine after this long.
Default 2 minutes.
.TP
\fB\-\-wait\fR \fIduration\fR
How long to wait for the person at the machine to confirm.
Default 5 minutes, the machine's own window.
.TP
\fB\-\-discovery\fR \fIURL\fR
The discovery server to look the machine up on, instead of the one in the link or the public one.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR
A file with the discovery settings, as for
.BR unlock .
.TP
\fB\-\-machines\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The file that names the machines; see
.BR machines .
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The long-term key, or where its record is; as for
.BR init .
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity; as for
.BR init .
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-addr\fR \fIhost:port\fR
Dial this address directly, bypassing relay and discovery.
For testing only.
.SS init
Generates a new long-term key and this key holder's transport identity, and
writes them under
.IR <user\ config\ dir> /synctang\-keyholder/
(see
.BR FILES ).
It then prints the public key and the transport ID, the same two lines as
.BR export\-pubkey ,
and the
.B synctang\-unlocker enrol
command to run on the machine, with those values filled in.
It refuses to overwrite an existing key, because the volumes enrolled against it
could no longer be opened.
The transport identity is always kept if it already exists, even with
.BR \-\-force :
a new one would change this key holder's Device ID and break every machine that
authorises it.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
Where to write the long-term private key.
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Replace an existing key, held by whichever store.
The old copy is removed from its store if the new key is kept in another, so that no replaced key
is left behind.
.TP
\fB\-\-store\fR \fIstore\fR
Where to keep the long-term key:
.BR file ,
.BR secret\-service ,
.BR systemd\-creds ,
.B systemd\-creds:tpm2
or
.B auto
(the default).
.B file
is a plain file with mode 0600: it keeps out other users of the laptop and nothing else.
.B secret\-service
is GNOME Keyring over D-Bus (the Secret Service; no other keyring is supported), encrypted with a key that the
login unlocks (a keyring with a blank password protects nothing against a copy of its files), so a copy of the configuration directory is
useless without the login password;
it is unlocked for the whole session, so software running as you can ask for it, and it needs a session
bus, which plain ssh does not give. The item is bound to the absolute path of the key file (a symbolic link
and its target are two keys, and a key directory that moves leaves the item behind), and the library has no
way to ask whether it exists without reading it.
.B systemd\-creds
seals the key with
.BR systemd\-creds (1)
in your own scope, into a file beside the key file
.RB ( key.cred ):
it needs systemd 256 or later, not a desktop session, so it works over ssh; but root can unseal it,
software running as you can run the tool itself, and the key that unseals it is on the same disk, so an image of
the disk of a laptop that is not encrypted gives both.
.B systemd\-creds:tpm2
also seals it to this machine's TPM (it refuses where there is none, and keeps its blob in
.IR key.tpm2.cred ;
not run on a machine with a TPM), which makes a copy of the disk useless without it, and ties the key to that
machine: it cannot be moved, and may be lost if the TPM or its measured state changes, so keep a backup of the
key some other way.
.B auto
never chooses these two; they are for those who ask.
.B auto
is the keyring when there is one and a plain file, with a warning that says why, when there is not.
A store named explicitly is never replaced by another when it is not there: the command fails.
The choice is recorded beside the key file, in
.BR key.store ,
which holds no secret; the other commands follow it.
The record also holds the public key the key was made with, and every use of the key is checked against it: a
key that a store hands back and that is not the one that was made (another program wrote the item, a backup
was restored over it) is refused, with a message that says so.
.BR status
says what holds the key and whether it matches; with
.BR \-\-verbose ,
any command says where it read the key from.
.SS export\-pubkey
Prints the public key and the transport identity, to give to
.BR "synctang\-unlocker enrol" :
.PP
.nf
S: <hex public key>
transport\-id: <transport identity>
.fi
.PP
The transport identity is created if it does not exist yet.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The long-term private key.
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.SS unlock
Answers one machine's recovery attempt.
The argument is the machine's name, as listed by
.BR machines ,
or its transport identity, as printed by
.B synctang\-unlocker enrol
or
.BR "synctang\-unlocker status" .
A name also brings the discovery server that was recorded with it.
With no argument, the only machine known is unlocked; with several, the command asks which, and lists them.
A name that is not known, but is close to one that is, is answered with
.IR "did you mean" .
.PP
It prints the machine's name and identity and waits for Enter before
answering; Ctrl-C aborts.
The name is chosen by the machine and proves nothing, so compare the identity.
With
.B \-\-yes
the prompt is skipped.
It then answers once, waits to be told whether the machine could use the answer,
and prints
.B unlocked
only when it was.
If an attempt fails, another is made every two seconds until the timeout.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The long-term private key.
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip the approval prompt.
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
Give up dialling the machine after this long.
Default 2 minutes.
.TP
\fB\-\-machines\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The file that names the machines (see
.BR machines ).
Default: the file
.I machines
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-discovery\fR \fIURL\fR
The discovery server to look the machine up on, instead of the public one.
The machine must announce to the same one.
The URL may say how to trust the server, with
.B ?id=
or
.BR ?insecure ;
see
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5).
.TP
\fB\-\-discovery\-announce\fR \fIURL\fR
The discovery announce URL, if it differs from
.BR \-\-discovery .
Accepted so that the same two keys work here as on a machine; a key holder only
looks up and never announces, so it does not change how the machine is found.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR
A file with the
.B discovery
and
.B discovery\-announce
settings, in the format of
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5).
Default: the file
.I config
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ),
which need not exist.
A file named here must exist.
A
.B relay\-pool
line is refused: only the machine joins a relay, and a key holder learns it from
discovery.
Flags win over the file.
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-addr\fR \fIhost:port\fR
Dial this address directly, bypassing relay and discovery.
For testing only.
.SS status
Says where the long-term key is kept and what that does and does not protect against, in
plain words, and whether the key can be read: it loads the key, only to say so, and wipes it
at once.
It prints no secret, and changes nothing: it makes no key, no record and no transport identity.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
Where the key is, or, when it is kept elsewhere, where its record is
.RB ( key.store
beside it).
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's transport identity, whose Device ID is printed.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory.
.SS machines
The key holder's address book: a name for each machine it can unlock, so that
.B synctang\-keyholder unlock my\-machine
works without anybody remembering a transport identity.
With no subcommand, or
.BR list ,
it lists them.
.B add
gives a name to a transport identity, as printed by
.B synctang\-unlocker enrol
on the machine; an existing name is not changed to mean another machine without
.BR \-\-force .
.B remove
forgets a name only: the machine still lets this key holder unlock it.
A name is letters, digits, dots, dashes and underscores, and cannot be one that
could be taken for a transport identity.
.PP
The file is plain text, one machine to a line, and may be edited by hand:
.B name transport\-id
and optionally a discovery server URL; blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
It holds nothing secret.
.TP
\fB\-\-machines\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
Where the names are.
Default: the file
.I machines
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-discovery\fR \fIURL\fR
With
.BR add :
the discovery server this machine uses, if it is not the public one.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
With
.BR add :
let an existing name mean this machine instead.
.SS completion
Writes to standard output the completion script for the shell named: bash, zsh or fish.
The program makes the script from its own usage text and the flag help of each command, so it
names exactly the commands and flags of this version and cannot fall out of step with them.
The Debian package installs the three scripts under
.BR /usr/share/bash\-completion/completions ,
.B /usr/share/zsh/vendor\-completions
and
.BR /usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d .
Without the package, bash can load it from
.B ~/.bashrc
with
.BR "source <(synctang\-keyholder completion bash)" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded; for
.BR unlock ,
the machine reported that it used the answer.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed.
The reason is printed on standard error with the prefix
.BR synctang\-keyholder: .
For
.B unlock
this includes running out of time without a successful answer.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong: an unknown command or flag, a missing argument, or a
setting that is not valid.
.SH FILES
All under
.IR <user\ config\ dir> /synctang\-keyholder/ ,
which is
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/synctang\-keyholder/
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set and
.B ~/.config/synctang\-keyholder/
otherwise.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/key
The long-term private key, hex-encoded.
Mode 0600, in a directory created with mode 0700.
Whoever can read it can answer for this key holder, so keep it private and back
it up: without it, the volumes enrolled against it cannot be opened.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/machines
The names of the machines this key holder can unlock, one to a line: a name, a transport
identity and optionally a discovery server.
Holds nothing secret.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/key.store
Which store holds the key, and the public key it was made with: the store on the first line, such as
.BR secret\-service ,
and
.BI "public " hex
on the second.
It holds no secret.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/key.cred
The key sealed by
.BR systemd\-creds (1),
when that is the store;
.I key.tpm2.cred
for the TPM variant.
Mode 0600.
Useless away from the machine and account that sealed it.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/transport.pem
This key holder's transport identity, a certificate and key, whose Device ID the
machines authorise.
Mode 0600.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/config
Optional settings; see
.BR \-\-config .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the user config directory is.
Default
.BR ~/.config .
.TP
.B SYNCTANG_DEBUG
If set and not 0, the debug log of
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and not empty, no colour (see
.BR \-\-color ).
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
If set and not 0, colour even when the output is not a terminal, unless
.B \-\-color=never
or
.B NO_COLOR
says otherwise.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
A UTF-8 locale lets the output use symbols and emoji; any other gets plain words.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
gets no colour, symbols or spinner, and
.B linux
(the console) no symbols.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the key and read off what the machine needs:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder init
synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey
.fi
.PP
Unlock a machine:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder unlock <machine transport id>
.fi
.PP
Unlock through a private discovery server that has a self-signed certificate,
named by its Device ID:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder unlock \e
  \-\-discovery 'https://192.0.2.20:8443/v2/?id=<server Device ID>' \e
  <machine transport id>
.fi
.PP
Rotate the key and keep the transport identity, then enrol the new key:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder init \-\-force
synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR synctang\-unlocker (8),
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5)
